### What is the internal structure of LinkedList?

LinkedList is built from `Node` objects. Each Node holds three things: the element itself, a reference to the previous Node, and a reference to the next Node. The list also maintains two pointers: `first` (the head) and `last` (the tail).

Here is the simplified internal structure from OpenJDK:

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class SimpleLinkedList<E> {
    private int size;
    private Node<E> first; // head
    private Node<E> last;  // tail

    private static class Node<E> {
        E item;
        Node<E> next;
        Node<E> prev;

        Node(Node<E> prev, E element, Node<E> next) {
            this.item = element;
            this.prev = prev;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    public void addFirst(E e) {
        Node<E> f = first;
        Node<E> newNode = new Node<>(null, e, f);
        first = newNode;
        if (f == null) {
            last = newNode; // list was empty
        } else {
            f.prev = newNode;
        }
        size++;
    }

    public void addLast(E e) {
        Node<E> l = last;
        Node<E> newNode = new Node<>(l, e, null);
        last = newNode;
        if (l == null) {
            first = newNode; // list was empty
        } else {
            l.next = newNode;
        }
        size++;
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public E get(int index) {
        // Optimization: start from closer end
        Node<E> x;
        if (index < (size >> 1)) {
            x = first;
            for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) x = x.next;
        } else {
            x = last;
            for (int i = size - 1; i > index; i--) x = x.prev;
        }
        return x.item;
    }

    public int size() { return size; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleLinkedList<String> accounts = new SimpleLinkedList<>();
        accounts.addLast("SAV2001");
        accounts.addLast("CUR3001");
        accounts.addFirst("FD4001");
        System.out.println("Element at 0: " + accounts.get(0));
        System.out.println("Element at 2: " + accounts.get(2));
        System.out.println("Size: " + accounts.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Element at 0: FD4001
Element at 2: CUR3001
Size: 3
```

### How do add and remove operations work?

All operations at the head or tail are O(1) because they only rewire 2-3 pointers. No elements need to move.

Adding at the head: create a new node, point its `next` to the current `first`, update the old first's `prev` to the new node, and set `first` to the new node.

Removing from the head: save the first node's item, set `first` to the first node's `next`, clear the old first node's references (to help GC), and update the new first node's `prev` to null.

Adding or removing in the middle requires finding the position first, which is O(n). But once the position is found, the actual link/unlink is O(1).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class LinkedListOperationsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<String> txnQueue = new LinkedList<>();

        // addFirst / addLast: O(1)
        txnQueue.addLast("TXN-001: Deposit 50000");
        txnQueue.addLast("TXN-002: Withdraw 10000");
        txnQueue.addFirst("TXN-000: High priority transfer");
        System.out.println("Queue: " + txnQueue);

        // removeFirst: O(1) – process from front
        String processed = txnQueue.removeFirst();
        System.out.println("Processed: " + processed);

        // removeLast: O(1) – remove from back
        String removed = txnQueue.removeLast();
        System.out.println("Removed last: " + removed);

        // peekFirst / peekLast: O(1) – read without removing
        txnQueue.addLast("TXN-003: EMI payment");
        System.out.println("Peek first: " + txnQueue.peekFirst());
        System.out.println("Peek last: " + txnQueue.peekLast());

        // get(index): O(n) – must traverse
        txnQueue.addLast("TXN-004: Interest credit");
        txnQueue.addLast("TXN-005: Statement fee");
        System.out.println("get(1): " + txnQueue.get(1)); // traversal from head
    }
}
```

```text
Queue: [TXN-000: High priority transfer, TXN-001: Deposit 50000, TXN-002: Withdraw 10000]
Processed: TXN-000: High priority transfer
Removed last: TXN-002: Withdraw 10000
Peek first: TXN-001: Deposit 50000
Peek last: TXN-003: EMI payment
get(1): TXN-003: EMI payment
```

### How does LinkedList implement the Deque interface?

LinkedList implements both `List<E>` and `Deque<E>`. This means you can use it as a queue (FIFO), a stack (LIFO), or a double-ended queue.

| Deque method | What it does | Time |
|---|---|---|
| offerFirst(e) / addFirst(e) | Insert at head | O(1) |
| offerLast(e) / addLast(e) | Insert at tail | O(1) |
| pollFirst() / removeFirst() | Remove from head | O(1) |
| pollLast() / removeLast() | Remove from tail | O(1) |
| peekFirst() / getFirst() | Read head without removing | O(1) |
| peekLast() / getLast() | Read tail without removing | O(1) |
| push(e) | Insert at head (stack push) | O(1) |
| pop() | Remove from head (stack pop) | O(1) |

The `offer/poll/peek` variants return null on failure. The `add/remove/get` variants throw exceptions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class DequeUsageDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // As a FIFO queue (first in, first out)
        Deque<String> fifoQueue = new LinkedList<>();
        fifoQueue.offerLast("Customer Rajesh");
        fifoQueue.offerLast("Customer Priya");
        fifoQueue.offerLast("Customer Amit");

        System.out.println("Queue processing (FIFO):");
        while (!fifoQueue.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  Serving: " + fifoQueue.pollFirst());
        }

        // As a LIFO stack (last in, first out)
        Deque<String> undoStack = new LinkedList<>();
        undoStack.push("Created account SAV2001");
        undoStack.push("Deposited 50000");
        undoStack.push("Set up auto-pay");

        System.out.println("\nUndo stack (LIFO):");
        while (!undoStack.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  Undo: " + undoStack.pop());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Queue processing (FIFO):
  Serving: Customer Rajesh
  Serving: Customer Priya
  Serving: Customer Amit

Undo stack (LIFO):
  Undo: Set up auto-pay
  Undo: Deposited 50000
  Undo: Created account SAV2001
```

### How does the get(index) optimization work?

LinkedList optimizes `get(index)` by checking whether the index is in the first half or second half of the list. If `index < size/2`, it traverses forward from `first`. If `index >= size/2`, it traverses backward from `last`. This halves the average traversal time, but it is still O(n).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class GetOptimizationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<String> accounts = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) accounts.add("ACC" + i);

        // get(2): index < 5 (size/2) → traverse from head
        // head → ACC0 → ACC1 → ACC2 (3 hops)
        System.out.println("get(2): " + accounts.get(2));

        // get(8): index >= 5 → traverse from tail
        // tail → ACC9 → ACC8 (2 hops – faster than 9 hops from head)
        System.out.println("get(8): " + accounts.get(8));
    }
}
```

```text
get(2): ACC2
get(8): ACC8
```

### What is the ListIterator advantage?

The only scenario where LinkedList genuinely outperforms ArrayList is when you use a `ListIterator` to insert or remove elements during traversal. At the iterator's current position, `add()` and `remove()` are O(1) on LinkedList because they just rewire pointers. On ArrayList, these operations are O(n) because elements must shift.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class ListIteratorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Scenario: process transactions and insert audit entries inline
        LinkedList<String> transactions = new LinkedList<>(List.of(
            "TXN-001: Deposit",
            "TXN-002: Withdraw",
            "TXN-003: Transfer"
        ));

        ListIterator<String> it = transactions.listIterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String txn = it.next();
            // Insert audit entry after each transaction – O(1)!
            it.add("[AUDIT] Verified: " + txn);
        }

        transactions.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-001: Deposit
[AUDIT] Verified: TXN-001: Deposit
TXN-002: Withdraw
[AUDIT] Verified: TXN-002: Withdraw
TXN-003: Transfer
[AUDIT] Verified: TXN-003: Transfer
```

### What about memory overhead?

Each LinkedList node is a separate Java object on the heap with the following layout (64-bit JVM with compressed oops):

- Object header: 12 bytes
- `item` reference: 4 bytes
- `next` reference: 4 bytes
- `prev` reference: 4 bytes
- Padding: 8 bytes (to align to 8-byte boundary)

Total per node: 32 bytes. Some JVMs may use 40 bytes depending on alignment.

For 1 million elements: LinkedList uses approximately 32-40 MB just for nodes, plus the elements themselves. ArrayList uses approximately 4 MB for the reference array.

The scattered allocation also means more work for the garbage collector, because it must track 1 million individual Node objects instead of one large array.

### What are common mistakes with LinkedList?

#### Mistake 1: Using get(index) in a loop

```java
// WRONG – O(n^2) because each get(i) is O(i)
LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) list.add("ACC" + i);

for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    String acc = list.get(i); // O(i) traversal each time!
}
// Total: O(1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n) = O(n^2)

// CORRECT – use iterator (O(n) total)
for (String acc : list) {
    // O(1) per iteration step
}
```

#### Mistake 2: Choosing LinkedList for a queue without considering ArrayDeque

```java
// SUBOPTIMAL – LinkedList has memory overhead and cache misses
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();

// BETTER – ArrayDeque is faster for both queue and stack
Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
```

### What about thread safety?

LinkedList is not thread-safe. For concurrent access:

- Use `Collections.synchronizedList(new LinkedList<>())` for simple thread safety.
- Use `ConcurrentLinkedDeque` for a lock-free concurrent deque.
- Use `LinkedBlockingDeque` for a bounded blocking deque (producer-consumer pattern).

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **No random access marker.** LinkedList does not implement `RandomAccess`. Algorithms in `Collections` check for this marker: `Collections.binarySearch()` uses an iterator-based approach for LinkedList instead of the index-based approach used for ArrayList. This makes binarySearch O(n) on LinkedList regardless.

- **Null elements.** LinkedList allows null elements, including multiple nulls. Be careful with `remove(null)` – it removes the first null found.

- **Serialization.** LinkedList has custom serialization that writes each element sequentially, not the node objects. The deserialized list creates fresh nodes. This makes serialization format efficient.

- **descendingIterator().** LinkedList provides `descendingIterator()` for backward traversal without the overhead of a ListIterator.

- **GC pressure.** Each `add()` creates a new Node object. In a high-throughput banking system adding millions of elements, this creates GC pressure. ArrayList creates no objects during add (unless resizing). This is another reason ArrayList is preferred.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the time complexity of get(500) on a LinkedList with 1000 elements?" – O(500), traverses from head. With 600: O(400), traverses from tail.
- "Can you implement a stack using LinkedList?" – Yes, push = addFirst, pop = removeFirst.
- "Why is ArrayDeque faster than LinkedList for queue operations?" – Contiguous circular array, cache-friendly, no node object creation.
- "What happens when you call get(index) on a LinkedList with a negative index?" – Throws IndexOutOfBoundsException.
